  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM MDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nUpper high pressure will continue to spin broadly over northern \nNM and the TX/OK Panhandle today through Thursday. Upper high \nbegins shifting towards the Four Corners region Friday and \nthroughout the weekend. Expect thunderstorm chances each afternoon\nwith a focus over the area mountains (Gila region and Sacramento \nMtns) and western locations bordering Arizona. Tuesday looks like \nit could have an uptick in storm activity in southwestern NM \nTuesday evening. CAMs are suggesting a large complex of storms in \nnorthwest Mexico which will push in better moisture to the \nsouthwestern portions of the CWA later tonight. This push of \nmoisture may help set the stage for Tuesday afternoon and evening.\nLooks like an area of surface convergence is possible as well. \nAreas of flash flooding will be possible. 0-6km shear values in \nthis area will be around 30-35KT which is sufficient for some \norganized cells. Wednesday and onward we keep our storm chances \nwith a focus over the area mountains and western locations. \n\nAs far as temperatures go, the area will remain HOT the next 7 days. \nTemperatures will generally be 7-10 degrees above normal. A Heat \nAdvisory is in effect for several lowland locations including Las \nCruces, Hatch, the El Paso metro, and the lower Valley today and \ntomorrow. Triple digit readings will be common across the lowlands \nfor the week ahead. May need to extend the Heat Advisory potentially \nthrough Friday. High temperatures will be close or will reach Heat \nAdvisory criteria (max temperatures greater than or equal to 105F) \nduring this time. This will be an extended heat wave, so please take \nthe heat seriously.",
